<script>(function(){var P=[["Mandaeism","/religion/mandaeism","Religion","c. 100 CE – present","Mandaeism is a Gnostic, monotheistic and ethnic religion of the Mandaeans, centred on the Great Life, Hayyi Rabbi, and on John the Baptist, practised mainly in southern Iraq and Khuzestan and known for its frequent baptisms in flowing water."],["Islamism","/ideology/islamism","Ideology","c. 1922 CE – present","Islamism is a range of religious and political movements, taking shape after the fall of the Ottoman Empire, that hold Islam to be innately political and seek sharia, pan-Islamic unity and Islamic states, through parties, social work or armed revolt."],["Protestant Reformation","/movement/protestant-reformation","Movement","1517 CE – 1648 CE","The religious movement, begun by Martin Luther in 1517, that broke the unity of Western Christianity, created the Lutheran, Reformed, Anglican and Anabaptist churches and reshaped European politics and society."],["Records of the Grand Historian","/work/records-of-the-grand-historian","Great work","c. 110 BCE – present","The Records of the Grand Historian, or Shiji, is a Chinese history written in the late 2nd and early 1st centuries BCE by Sima Qian, building on his father's work, and the first of the Twenty-Four Histories of imperial China."],["One-party state","/government/one-party-state","Form of government","c. 1918 CE – present","A state in which one political party holds a legal or practical monopoly of power and others are banned or kept subordinate: the form of communist, fascist and many post-colonial regimes since 1918."],["Celtic religion","/religion/celtic-religion","Religion","c. 550 BCE – c. 600 CE","Celtic religion was the polytheistic religion of the ancient Celtic peoples of Iron Age Europe, known from archaeology, Greek and Roman writers and medieval Irish and Welsh texts, and remembered for its druids, sacred groves and offerings in water."],["Conservatism","/ideology/conservatism","Ideology","c. 1790 CE – present","Conservatism is a cultural, social and political philosophy that seeks to preserve traditional institutions, customs and values; it took shape in Edmund Burke’s opposition to the French Revolution in the 1790s and became one of the three major modern ideologies."],["New Culture Movement","/movement/new-culture-movement","Movement","1915 CE – c. 1925 CE","The New Culture Movement was a progressive sociopolitical movement in China in the 1910s and 1920s, in which scholars around the magazine New Youth criticised Confucian tradition and urged a vernacular literature, science and democracy."],["Hebrew Bible","/work/hebrew-bible","Great work","c. 800 BCE – present","The Hebrew Bible is the canonical collection of Hebrew scriptures, the Torah, Nevi'im and Ketuvim, composed and edited by the Israelites over several hundred years; its authoritative Jewish form was fixed in the Masoretic Text of the 7th to 10th centuries CE."],["Dictatorship","/government/dictatorship","Form of government","c. 501 BCE – present","Rule by one leader or a small group holding near-absolute power, unchecked by law or free elections: named after the Roman Republic’s emergency magistrate, it became the modern name for unaccountable rule."],["Druze faith","/religion/druze-faith","Religion","1017 CE – present","The Druze faith is an esoteric monotheistic religion that arose in Fatimid Cairo in 1017 from the Ismāʿīlī branch of Shiʿa Islam, known for its belief in reincarnation, its secret teachings and its closed community in the Levant."],["Capitalism","/ideology/capitalism","Ideology","c. 1500 CE – present","Capitalism is an economic system based on private ownership of the means of production and its use for profit; emerging from 16th-century English agriculture and European mercantilism, it became dominant with the Industrial Revolution and spread worldwide."],["Bhakti movement","/movement/bhakti-movement","Movement","c. 600 CE – c. 1700 CE","The devotional movement that began among Tamil poet-saints around the 6th century and spread across India over a thousand years, teaching love of a personal god in songs in the people’s own languages."],["Colosseum","/work/colosseum","Great work","c. 72 CE – present","The Colosseum is the elliptical amphitheatre in the centre of Rome, begun under Vespasian in 72 and completed under Titus in 80, the largest ancient amphitheatre ever built and a lasting symbol of Imperial Rome."],["Nomadic confederation","/government/nomadic-confederation","Form of government","209 BCE – 1757 CE","A nomadic confederation joins herding tribes under one ruling clan and its khan; from the Xiongnu to the Mongols such steppe powers built the largest land empires and drew tribute from China and Constantinople."],["Islam","/religion/islam","Religion","610 CE – present","The faith proclaimed by the prophet Muhammad in 7th-century Arabia: submission to the one God, revealed in the Quran, which spread within a century from Spain to Central Asia and is today the religion of about two billion people."],["Socialism","/ideology/socialism","Ideology","1827 CE – present","Socialism is a family of political and economic ideas calling for social ownership of the means of production; it arose in early 19th-century Europe in answer to industrial capitalism and shaped communist and social democratic governments and the welfare state."],["Self-Strengthening Movement","/movement/self-strengthening-movement","Movement","1861 CE – 1895 CE","A programme of reform in Qing China from 1861 to 1895, led by court ministers and provincial governors, that adopted Western weapons, ships and industry to save the dynasty while keeping the Confucian order."],["Arthashastra","/work/arthashastra","Great work","c. 150 BCE – present","The Arthashastra, or Kautilya's Arthashastra, is an ancient Indian Sanskrit treatise on statecraft, politics, economic policy and military strategy, compiled and revised over centuries and most likely complete between the 1st and 3rd century CE."],["Direct democracy","/government/direct-democracy","Form of government","508 BCE – present","Government in which the citizens themselves decide laws and policies, in an open assembly or by popular vote, instead of through elected representatives: practised in classical Athens and in Switzerland today."],["Caodaism","/religion/caodaism","Religion","1925 CE – present","Caodaism is a Vietnamese monotheistic syncretic religion, founded in Saigon in 1925 and officially established at Tây Ninh in 1926, that joins Confucian ethics, Taoist practice, Buddhist ideas of karma and rebirth and a hierarchical organisation from Catholicism."],["Marxism–Leninism","/ideology/marxism-leninism","Ideology","c. 1924 CE – present","Marxism–Leninism is a communist ideology built on Marx and Lenin and formulated under Stalin in the Soviet Union in the 1920s; it became the state ideology of the Soviet Union and of many communist states in the 20th century."],["Tanzimat","/movement/tanzimat","Movement","1839 CE – 1876 CE","The Tanzimat, or “Reorganization”, was the period of Ottoman reform from the Edict of Gülhane of 1839 to the constitution of 1876, which modernised the empire’s law, army and administration and promised equality to all its subjects."],["Great Wall of China","/work/great-wall-of-china","Great work","c. 700 BCE – present","A series of fortifications built along the historical northern borders of China from the 7th century BCE against nomadic groups of the Eurasian Steppe, joined in the Qin dynasty and best known from its Ming sections."],["Theocracy","/government/theocracy","Form of government","c. 515 BCE – present","Government in the name of God or the gods, by priests, a sacred ruler or religious law; the word was coined for ancient Judea, and the Papal States, Tibet under the Dalai Lamas and Iran are classic examples."],["Gnosticism","/religion/gnosticism","Religion","c. 150 CE – present","Gnosticism is a modern scholarly name for diverse religious systems that took shape by the mid-2nd century in the Mediterranean and Near East, centred on saving knowledge (gnosis) and a flawed creator; Mandaeism is counted as its surviving form."],["Legalism","/ideology/legalism","Ideology","c. 350 BCE – c. 200 CE","Legalism is the later name for a Chinese school of statecraft of the Warring States period, which sought a strong state through clear laws, administrative method, agriculture and war, and shaped the Qin empire and the bureaucracy of imperial China."],["Counter-Reformation","/movement/counter-reformation","Movement","1545 CE – 1648 CE","The Counter-Reformation, or Catholic Reformation, was the Catholic resurgence that answered the Protestant Reformation from the Council of Trent (1545–1563) to 1648, reforming the clergy, reaffirming Catholic doctrine and winning back much of Europe."],["Mogao Caves","/work/mogao-caves","Great work","c. 366 CE – present","The Mogao Caves are a system of Buddhist cave temples near Dunhuang in Gansu, China, begun in 366 CE and added to until the 14th century, known for their murals and sculptures and for the manuscripts of the Library Cave."],["Absolute monarchy","/government/absolute-monarchy","Form of government","c. 1500 CE – present","Rule by a hereditary monarch who holds supreme power unchecked by any constitution, parliament or court: the form claimed by the great monarchies of early modern Europe and kept by a handful of states today."],["Yoruba religion","/religion/yoruba-religion","Religion","c. 1000 CE – present","The traditional religion of the Yoruba of West Africa, centred on the sacred city of Ile-Ife, which worships the supreme god Olodumare and the orisha through sacrifice, music and the Ifa divination, and lives on in the Americas in Santería and Candomblé."],["Pacifism","/ideology/pacifism","Ideology","c. 1815 CE – present","Pacifism is the opposition to war or violence, ranging from the rejection of all warfare to the belief that disputes can be settled peacefully; it has ancient precedents, took organised form in the peace movements from 1815 and gained its name in 1901."],["Temperance movement","/movement/temperance-movement","Movement","c. 1820 CE – present","The temperance movement was a social movement, strongest in English-speaking, Scandinavian and Protestant countries from the 19th century, that urged moderation in or total abstinence from alcohol and, in many places, laws restricting or prohibiting its sale."],["Grand Canal of China","/work/grand-canal-of-china","Great work","c. 486 BCE – present","The Grand Canal is a system of linked canals joining rivers and lakes in North and East China, begun with the Han Canal in 486 BCE, completed by Emperor Yang of Sui in 609 and used as a north–south artery for some fourteen centuries."],["Aristocracy","/government/aristocracy","Form of government","c. 750 BCE – c. 1918 CE","Rule by a small privileged class claiming to be the best fitted to govern, in practice usually a hereditary nobility, from the early Greek city-states to the upper houses of Europe’s monarchies."],["Shinto","/religion/shinto","Religion","c. 300 BCE – present","The native religion of Japan, the worship of the kami, the sacred powers of nature, ancestors and heroes, at shrines across the islands, intertwined for a thousand years with Buddhism and made the creed of the modern state until 1945."],["Trotskyism","/ideology/trotskyism","Ideology","1905 CE – present","Trotskyism is the branch of Marxism and Leninism developed by Leon Trotsky, built on permanent revolution, workers' democracy and internationalism; it took shape in Russia from 1905 and lives on in small parties and internationals worldwide."],["Chartism","/movement/chartism","Movement","1838 CE – 1857 CE","Chartism was a working-class movement for political reform in the United Kingdom from 1838 to 1857, which petitioned Parliament in millions for the six points of the People’s Charter, above all a vote for every man."],["Mona Lisa","/work/mona-lisa","Great work","c. 1503 CE – present","The Mona Lisa is a half-length oil portrait on a poplar panel by Leonardo da Vinci, traditionally held to show Lisa del Giocondo, begun in Florence around 1503 and kept in the Louvre in Paris since 1797."],["Feudalism","/government/feudalism","Form of government","c. 800 CE – 1789 CE","Feudalism was the medieval European order in which lords granted fiefs to vassals for military service and loyalty, leaving public power divided among landholders; historians now debate whether the term fits."],["Hawaiian religion","/religion/hawaiian-religion","Religion","c. 800 CE – present","Hawaiian religion, also known as the kapu system, comprises the Indigenous beliefs and practices of Native Hawaiians: a polytheistic tradition with Kāne, Kū, Lono and Kanaloa among its chief gods, brought by Polynesian settlers between 500 and 1300 CE."],["Anarchism","/ideology/anarchism","Ideology","c. 1840 CE – present","Anarchism seeks to abolish the state, capitalism and all institutions of authority, coercion and hierarchy; it arose in 19th-century Europe with Proudhon, Bakunin and Kropotkin and flourished in workers’ struggles worldwide until the Spanish Civil War."],["Quakers","/movement/quakers","Movement","c. 1650 CE – present","The Quakers, or Religious Society of Friends, are a Christian movement that arose in mid-17th-century England and spread overseas, stressing the inward light, worship without clergy, and testimonies against oaths, war and slavery."],["The Canon of Medicine","/work/the-canon-of-medicine","Great work","1025 CE – present","The Canon of Medicine is an encyclopedia of medicine in five books compiled by Ibn Sina (Avicenna) and completed in 1025, which gathered the medical knowledge of the Islamic world and became a standard university textbook in Europe."],["Parliamentary democracy","/government/parliamentary-democracy","Form of government","c. 1721 CE – present","Democracy in which the government is formed from an elected parliament and stays in office only while it keeps parliament’s confidence: born in 18th-century Britain, now the usual form in Europe and the Commonwealth."],["Tenrikyo","/religion/tenrikyo","Religion","1838 CE – present","Tenrikyo is a Japanese new religion that arose in 1838 from the teachings of Nakayama Miki, called Oyasama by her followers, and is known for its worship of God the Parent, the pilgrimage to the Jiba and the practice of hinokishin."],["Republicanism","/ideology/republicanism","Ideology","c. 500 BCE – present","Republicanism is a political ideology which holds that sovereignty resides in the people and their elected representatives rather than a hereditary monarch; rooted in republican Rome and revived in the Italian city-states, it shaped the American and French Revolutions."],["Zionism","/movement/zionism","Movement","1882 CE – present","Zionism is a nationalist movement that emerged in late 19th-century Europe to establish a Jewish homeland in Palestine, roughly the Land of Israel of Judaism; it was the main force behind the creation of the State of Israel in 1948."],["Lotus Sutra","/work/lotus-sutra","Great work","c. 50 CE – present","The Lotus Sūtra is a Mahāyāna Buddhist scripture by unknown Indian authors, composed in phases from about the 1st century CE, and the main text of the Tiantai, Tendai and Nichiren schools of East Asia."],["Federation","/government/federation","Form of government","1789 CE – present","A state whose constitution divides power between a central government and self-governing member states, neither able to abolish the other: pioneered by the United States in 1789, now home to some 40 per cent of humanity."],["Ancient Greek religion","/religion/ancient-greek-religion","Religion","c. 1400 BCE – c. 500 CE","The religion of the ancient Greeks, who worshipped Zeus and the Olympian gods, heroes and local powers with sacrifice, festivals, games and oracles, and whose myths became a foundation of Western art and literature."],["Utilitarianism","/ideology/utilitarianism","Ideology","c. 1780 CE – present","Utilitarianism is a family of ethical theories holding that actions should maximise happiness and well-being for all affected; it took shape in 18th-century England with Jeremy Bentham and has been applied to economics, law, poverty and animal ethics."],["Women's suffrage movement","/movement/womens-suffrage-movement","Movement","1848 CE – 1971 CE","The century-long campaign, from the Seneca Falls Convention of 1848 to the Swiss vote of 1971, that won women the right to vote and to stand for election in most of the world’s states."],["Book of the Dead","/work/book-of-the-dead","Great work","c. 1700 BCE – present","The Book of the Dead is an ancient Egyptian funerary text of magic spells, written mostly on papyrus from the beginning of the New Kingdom to around 50 BCE, meant to guide the dead through the underworld into the afterlife."],["Republic","/government/republic","Form of government","c. 509 BCE – present","Government without a monarch, in which the state is the public’s affair, run by elected magistrates and councils under law: born in ancient Rome, revived in medieval Italy and the Netherlands, now the usual form."],["Inca religion","/religion/inca-religion","Religion","c. 1200 CE – c. 1572 CE","Inca religion was the polytheist system of beliefs and rites of the Inca Empire, or Tawantinsuyu, which grew out of older Andean traditions and centred on the Sun god Inti, the creator Viracocha, sacred places called wak'a and the worship of royal ancestors."],["Hindutva","/ideology/hindutva","Ideology","1923 CE – present","Hindutva is a Hindu nationalist ideology, formulated by Vinayak Damodar Savarkar in Essentials of Hindutva in 1923, that grounds Indian nationhood in a shared Hindu culture and guides the RSS, the VHP and the ruling Bharatiya Janata Party."],["Meiji Restoration","/movement/meiji-restoration","Movement","1853 CE – 1889 CE","The Meiji Restoration was the political upheaval that ended the Tokugawa shogunate and restored power to the Emperor of Japan in 1868, then abolished the domains and the samurai class and set Japan on a course of Westernisation and industrialisation."],["Mosque–Cathedral of Córdoba","/work/mosque-cathedral-of-cordoba","Great work","785 CE – present","The Mosque–Cathedral of Córdoba is the building begun in 785 as the Great Mosque of Córdoba for the Umayyad emir Abd al-Rahman I, expanded until the late 10th century and made a cathedral in 1236, now the city's cathedral."],["Elective monarchy","/government/elective-monarchy","Form of government","633 CE – present","A monarchy in which the ruler is chosen by election rather than by birth, by princes, nobles, councils or fellow rulers: the form of the Holy Roman Empire and Poland–Lithuania, and of the papacy and Malaysia today."]],d=Math.floor(Date.now()/864e5),p=P[d%P.length],a=document.getElementById("hub-pick");if(a){a.href=p[1];var im=document.createElement("img");im.src="/logos/thumbs/"+encodeURIComponent(p[0]).replace(/'/g,"%27")+".png";im.alt="";im.width=64;im.height=64;a.querySelector(".hub-pick-img").appendChild(im);a.querySelector(".hub-pick-k").textContent="Today\u2019s pick \u00b7 "+p[2]+(p[3]?" \u00b7 "+p[3]:"");a.querySelector(".hub-pick-n").textContent=p[0];a.querySelector(".hub-pick-d").textContent=p[4];}var b=document.getElementById("hub-surprise"),P=["/religion/","/ideology/","/movement/","/work/","/government/"];if(b)b.addEventListener("click",function(){function go(){if(typeof SEARCH_PROFILES==="undefined")return;var a=SEARCH_PROFILES.filter(function(r){return P.some(function(p){return r[1].indexOf(p)===0})});if(a.length)location.href=a[Math.floor(Math.random()*a.length)][1]}if(typeof SEARCH_PROFILES!=="undefined")return go();var sc=document.createElement("script");sc.src=(typeof SEARCH_PROFILES_SRC!=="undefined"?SEARCH_PROFILES_SRC:"/search-profiles.js");sc.onload=go;sc.onerror=go;document.head.appendChild(sc)});})();</script>
